    <article class="editorial-article">

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>The Core Fitment Dispute: Historical Parity vs Progressive Real Wage Growth</h2>
        <p>
          As discussions surrounding the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission (8th CPC)</strong> gain momentum, the focal point of negotiations between employee federations and the Government of India centers on a specific numerical choice: <strong>2.57 versus 2.86</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number <strong>2.57</strong> carries historical weight. It is the exact uniform fitment multiplier recommended by Justice A.K. Mathur in the 7th Central Pay Commission report and subsequently approved by the Union Cabinet in 2016. Proponents of 2.57 argue that maintaining this ratio preserves institutional continuity, ensures predictable budgetary modeling, and matches the wage-expansion ratios applied across earlier decennial transitions.
        </p>
        <p>
          Conversely, the number <strong>2.86</strong> represents the target multiplier formulated by the National Council (Staff Side) of the Joint Consultative Machinery (NC-JCM), the All India Railwaymen’s Federation (AIRF), and defence civilian confederations. The staff side asserts that retail inflation, elevated healthcare costs, and expanding private-sector compensation benchmarks require a more progressive multiplier to deliver a meaningful real wage improvement.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Deconstructing the 2.57 Multiplier: How the 7th CPC Reached This Number</h2>
        <p>
          To evaluate whether a 2.57 fitment factor remains appropriate for the 8th Pay Commission, it is essential to examine its mathematical formulation under the 7th CPC:
        </p>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <p>
            <strong>7th CPC Multiplier Derivation:</strong> [ 1.00 (Base Pay) &plus; 1.25 (125% Merged DA) ] &times; 1.1422 (14.22% Real Wage Hike) = <strong>2.57</strong>
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          When the 7th CPC established pay matrices effective January 1, 2016:
        </p>
        <ul class="editorial-list">
          <li>Dearness Allowance had reached exactly <strong>125%</strong>, establishing an inflation-neutral consolidation baseline of 2.25 times the 6th CPC basic pay.</li>
          <li>The Commission evaluated that absorbing DA alone would leave real purchasing power unchanged. It therefore incorporated a <strong>14.22% net real wage increase</strong>.</li>
          <li>Applying this percentage across the consolidated base yielded the uniform 2.57 factor, which raised the minimum basic pay from ₹7,000 (PB-1 plus Grade Pay ₹1,800) to <strong>₹18,000 per month</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If applied to the 8th Pay Commission, a 2.57 factor would take the existing ₹18,000 minimum basic pay and lift it to <strong>₹46,260 per month</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Deconstructing the 2.86 Demand: The Living Wage and Aykroyd Norms</h2>
        <p>
          The demand for a <strong>2.86 fitment factor</strong> is not arbitrary. It is derived directly from the application of the <strong>Dr. Wallace Aykroyd nutritional formula</strong>, as affirmed by the 15th Indian Labour Conference (ILC) and modified by the Supreme Court of India in the <em>Reptakos Brett</em> judgment:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>Why Unions Demand 2.86:</h4>
          <p>
            Employee federations calculated the retail cost of a standard physiological diet (2,700 calories per adult across three consumption units), 72 yards of clothing, housing rent at 10%, fuel and lighting at 20%, and the mandatory 25% allocation for education, medical outlays, and social obligations.
          </p>
          <p>
            Based on current retail price indices across major Indian urban and semi-urban labor centers, the cost of this minimum basket totals approximately <strong>₹51,480 per month</strong> for a family of four. Dividing this target wage of ₹51,480 by the existing Level 1 basic pay of ₹18,000 produces:
          </p>
          <p class="formula-box">
            ₹51,480 &divide; ₹18,000 = <strong>2.86</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Thus, employee unions argue that adopting 2.86 is necessary to satisfy statutory minimum living wage standards.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

